\subsection{Time budget}

The practical advantage is compute.  PLS-HPO has a median total time of
984.90 s per completed run and an observed successful-row total of 162.4 h.
AOM-PLS (simple), implemented as \texttt{AOM-compact-cv5}, has a median total
time of 1.76 s, and AOM-PLS (best) has a median total time of 1.94 s and an
observed total of 0.2 h.  On median total time, AOM-PLS (best) is about 508
times faster than PLS-HPO.  In runner-level search units, the contrast is
3000 trial fits per PLS-HPO dataset/seed versus 45 compact-bank CV fits for
each AOM-PLS row.

Ridge is different because AOM-Ridge (best), the Blender selector, is heavier.
It still improves over Ridge-HPO on the completed overlap, and its median
total time is 960.71 s compared with 2024.55 s for Ridge-HPO.  AOM-Ridge
(simple), implemented as \texttt{AOMRidge-global-compact-none}, is much
lighter, with median fit time 21.60 s and 450 operator-alpha cells.  The
result is not that every AOM selector is instant; it is that strict-linear
preprocessing can be represented as a model geometry rather than as a blind
external pipeline loop.

